\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#experimental}{\figure{lifecycle-experimental.svg}{options: alt='[Experimental]'}}}{\strong{[Experimental]}}
Adds an event date, an event indicator, a censoring date and a time for
each post-transplant outcome: patient death, all-cause and death-censored
graft failure, malignancy and acute rejection. The follow-up forms are
read from the matching follow-up file (\code{TX_KI} to \code{TXF_KI}, and so on)
with \code{\link[=load_srtr_file]{load_srtr_file()}}, for the cohort's \code{TRR_ID}s only. Defaults follow
the SRTR program-specific report methods where SRTR gives a rule; the
arguments switch to other common choices.
}
\details{
It accepts all eight transplant files (\code{TX_KI}, \code{TX_LI}, \code{TX_PA},
\code{TX_KP}, \code{TX_HR}, \code{TX_LU}, \code{TX_HL} and \code{TX_IN}), whose columns the SRTR
data dictionary lists. So far only \code{TX_LI} has been checked on a real
release; check the others on your own files (see the \emph{Checking sRtr on
your own SAFs} guide).

\code{REC_DEATH_DT_COMPOSITE} keeps every death date from every source. Only the
event and censoring columns (\code{REC_DEATH_DT_COMPOSITE_BINARY}, \verb{_CENSOR},
\verb{_days}) depend on \code{death_window}. Counting deaths found after last
follow-up while censoring survivors at last follow-up would overstate
mortality (#18).

Two graft failure outcomes are added. \strong{All-cause graft failure}
(\code{GRAFT_FAIL_ALL_CAUSE_DT}, or \code{GRAFT_FAIL_ALL_CAUSE_DT_KI} and
\code{GRAFT_FAIL_ALL_CAUSE_DT_PA} for \code{TX_KP}) follows the SRTR
program-specific reports: the graft fails at the first of its graft
failure date, retransplant (see \code{retransplant}) and death from any source
(\code{REC_DEATH_DT_COMPOSITE}). The graft is censored at its last follow-up
date, and an event after that date is not counted. Heart grafts
(\code{TX_HR}) follow SRTR's heart rule instead: only death and retransplant
count, the graft failure date is not used, and follow-up is not censored
at the last follow-up date but ends where death ascertainment ends (see
\code{death_window}). The date column holds the first event even when it is
not counted, and \verb{_SOURCE} says which it was (\code{"graft_failure"},
\code{"retransplant"} or \code{"death"}; on the same day, in that order).

As in the SRTR reports, a graft is censored on the day of transplant,
with no event, when any of its graft failure, retransplant or death
dates falls before the transplant date (\verb{_SOURCE} is then
\code{"before_transplant"}, and a later event is not used in its place), or
when its last follow-up date is missing or before the transplant date.
Its time is then the one-day offset that \code{\link[=srtr_time_to_event]{srtr_time_to_event()}} adds.

\strong{Death-censored graft failure} (\code{TFL_GRAFT_DT}, or \code{TFL_GRAFT_DT_KI} and
\code{TFL_GRAFT_DT_PA} for \code{TX_KP}) is not an SRTR report definition, and
does not depend on \code{death_window}: a graft failure dated after the recipient's death from any source (\code{REC_DEATH_DT_COMPOSITE})
is not counted, and a graft without a failure is censored at the earlier of
its last follow-up date and death. Death with a functioning graft is a
censoring time, not a graft failure. A graft failure dated after the last
follow-up date but before death is still counted, at its own date.

First rejection (\code{FIRST_REJ_DATE}, and \code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_PA} for \code{TX_KP})
counts a treated rejection (code 1 of format \code{ACREJEP}, raw or labelled)
during the transplant admission (\code{REC_ACUTE_REJ_EPISODE}, or
\code{REC_ACUTE_REJ_EPISODE_PA}) first, else the first follow-up form reporting
one. An admission rejection is dated at discharge, a proxy: the transplant
date plus \code{REC_POSTX_LOS}, else \code{REC_DISCHRG_DT}; a date before the
transplant is not used. \code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_SOURCE} (\code{"admission_los"},
\code{"admission_discharge"}, \code{"admission_undated"} or \code{"follow_up"}) says which
rule applied (\code{"follow_up_undated"} is described below). Here a missing
date does not always mean no event: an
admission rejection with no usable date keeps \verb{_BINARY = 1} with a missing
censoring date and time, with a warning, so it drops out of time-to-event
models instead of counting as rejection-free. A later follow-up rejection
stays in \code{FIRST_FU_REJ_DATE} (\code{FIRST_FU_REJ_DATE_KI} and
\code{FIRST_FU_REJ_DATE_PA} for \code{TX_KP}, with \code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_PA_SOURCE} for the
pancreas) and is never used in its place. A date is unusable when it falls
before the transplant; with no transplant date, the discharge date is kept,
and the elapsed time is missing.

Malignancy (\code{FIRST_FU_MALIG_DATE}) and rejection count only inside the
window the recipient or graft is followed for: from the transplant date
up to the earlier of the last follow-up date and death from any source
(\code{REC_DEATH_DT_COMPOSITE}), both inclusive, whatever \code{death_window} is. Malignancy uses the patient's last
follow-up (for \code{TX_KP}, the later of the two graft dates); rejection uses
the graft's own (\code{TFL_LAFUDATEKI} or \code{TFL_LAFUDATEPA} for \code{TX_KP}). Without
an event, the recipient is censored at the end of the window. A report
dated after it is not an event, but its date stays in the date column, so
\code{!is.na(FIRST_FU_MALIG_DATE) & FIRST_FU_MALIG_DATE_BINARY == 0} finds the
reports left out. A report dated before the transplant is not used:
events are timed from the first report on or after the transplant date,
while \code{FIRST_FU_MALIG_DATE} and \code{FIRST_FU_REJ_DATE} keep the first report
of all, for auditing (\code{FIRST_REJ_DATE} is the date used). With no last
follow-up date the window's end is unknown: death is not used in its place,
and the time is missing.

A malignancy or rejection reported only on follow-up forms with no date
(status \code{"event"} with a missing date in \code{\link[=add_txf_outcomes]{add_txf_outcomes()}}) is treated
like an undated admission rejection: \verb{_BINARY = 1} with a missing
censoring date and time, with a warning, so it does not add event-free
time. For rejection, \code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_SOURCE} is then
\code{"follow_up_undated"}.

Follow-up dates are report dates (see \code{\link[=add_txf_outcomes]{add_txf_outcomes()}}). A report
dated on the day of death may come from the form filed at death, so the
event may have happened earlier. \code{FIRST_FU_MALIG_DATE_DEATH_DAY} and
\code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_DEATH_DAY} (and \code{FIRST_REJ_DATE_PA_DEATH_DAY}) are \code{TRUE}
when the date is the day of death, \code{FALSE} when it is another day, and
\code{NA} with no date, so you can run a sensitivity analysis without those
events. Such reports count as events by default when follow-up covers the
day of death. The status columns from \code{\link[=add_txf_outcomes]{add_txf_outcomes()}} say what the
forms report, before the window is applied.
}
